-        (Note: `/` denotes integer division) [S1, unofficial].
+### Algorithm Steps
+1.  **Padding:** Allocate Voxel Memory with dimensions rounded up to the nearest multiple of 32 in each axis. Copy the source volume data into this buffer, padding with zeros or boundary values as needed [S1, unofficial].
+2.  **Miniblock Formation:** Group voxels into 2x2x2 miniblocks. Store each miniblock sequentially in memory to exploit SDRAM burst capabilities [S1, unofficial].
+3.  **Module Distribution:** Distribute miniblocks across the four memory modules such that any group of four adjacent miniblocks (along any axis) resides in separate modules. This is achieved by the module index formula above [S1, unofficial].
+4.  **Bank Skewing:** Within each memory module, skew the position of miniblocks so that adjacent miniblocks map to different SDRAM banks. This prevents bank conflicts during back-to-back accesses. This is achieved by the bank index formula above [S1, unofficial].
+5.  **Reordering (De-skewing):** During rendering, the view direction changes. The Voxel Memory controllers read miniblocks in their stored order. A global de-skewing network re-shuffles the four miniblocks output by the memory interfaces so that the left-most voxel along the x-axis flows down the leftmost pipeline, and adjacent x-voxels flow down neighboring pipelines [S1, unofficial].
+6.  **Slice Buffering:** The de-skewed miniblocks are written into voxel slice buffers in the rendering pipelines. The interface reads two slices of voxels at a time to populate the buffers [S1, unofficial].
+7.  **Gradient Computation:** The slice buffers store three slices worth of data to allow z-gradient computation using central differences [S1, unofficial].
 
